---
import { ISSUES_URL } from "../config";
import LegalPage from "../layouts/LegalPage.astro";
---

<LegalPage
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="How Social Media Agent handles your data: it stays on your computer."
  updated="September 28, 2026"
>
  <p>
    Social Media Agent is a desktop app for planning, drafting and scheduling
    Instagram Reels. It runs on your computer and has no servers or accounts of
    its own. This policy explains what data it handles and where that data goes.
  </p>

  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Your drafts, chats, contents and schedule are stored only on your
      computer.
    </li>
    <li>
      What you type in a drafting chat goes to OpenAI through the Codex CLI,
      under your own ChatGPT account.
    </li>
    <li>We don't collect analytics, telemetry or crash reports.</li>
    <li>The app checks GitHub for updates, and GitHub sees those requests.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Data stored on your computer</h2>
  <p>
    Everything you create in the app (draft chats, draft documents, contents and
    your posting schedule) is saved in a local database in your user profile. On
    Windows that is
    <code>%APPDATA%\Social Media Agent\data</code>. We never receive a copy. You
    can export or restore it from Settings, and delete it by removing that
    folder.
  </p>
  <p>
    Error and crash logs are also kept locally, in
    <code>%APPDATA%\Social Media Agent\logs</code>. They are never uploaded. If
    you report a problem, you choose whether to share them.
  </p>

  <h2>AI drafting through Codex and OpenAI</h2>
  <p>
    Drafting uses the Codex CLI (made by OpenAI), which the app runs on your
    computer and signs in with your ChatGPT account. When you send a message in
    a draft, or convert a draft into content, that text and the conversation so
    far are sent to OpenAI to generate a reply. OpenAI handles that data under
    its own terms and privacy policy, and it counts toward your ChatGPT plan's
    usage limits. We do not see or store it anywhere except on your computer.
  </p>
  <p>
    Signing in happens in your web browser on OpenAI's site. The app never sees
    your password. Codex stores its sign-in and its own conversation history in
    your user folder (<code>~/.codex</code>). Deleting a draft in the app also
    deletes its Codex conversation.
  </p>

  <h2>Updates</h2>
  <p>
    The installed app checks for new versions on GitHub, where releases are
    published, a few times a day and downloads updates from there. Like any
    website, GitHub receives your IP address and basic request information when
    this happens. See GitHub's privacy statement for how it handles that.
  </p>

  <h2>This website</h2>
  <p>
    This site is a static page. It sets no cookies and runs no analytics or
    tracking scripts. The company hosting it may keep standard server logs, such
    as IP address and browser type, to operate and secure the site.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>
    The app requires a ChatGPT account, so it is subject to OpenAI's minimum age
    requirements. It is not directed at children.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If how the app handles data changes, we will update this page and its date
    before the change ships.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about privacy? <a href={ISSUES_URL}>Open an issue on GitHub</a>.
  </p>
</LegalPage>
